Build against a modern toolchain and glibc.

Three changes, none of them to Broadcom's logic:

  * Werror stops being fatal. A decade of new gcc diagnostics arrive at once
    on code that cannot be revised upstream, so the warnings stay and stop
    failing the build. Same for the format-security variant.

  * The nsl library is dropped. It was folded into glibc years ago and no
    longer exists separately, so linking against it fails outright.

  * muldefs is added, because the SDK defines some symbols in more than one
    archive and the linker of its era accepted that by default.

Paths carry the sdk-6.5.24 component because the archive holds ten SDK
versions side by side, not one tree. This is pinned to 6.5.24: it is the
version proven on the 7050SX2, and the same repairs are spelled differently in
other versions, so it should fail loudly rather than apply loosely elsewhere.

Note for anyone editing this file: no line of this preamble may begin with a
plus or minus, or patch reads it as diff content and rejects the whole file.

--- a/sdk-6.5.24/make/Makefile.unix-user
+++ b/sdk-6.5.24/make/Makefile.unix-user
@@ -56,7 +56,7 @@
 OSTYPE = LINUX
 
 # Linux
-LIBS =-lnsl -pthread -lm -lrt
+LIBS =-Wl,-z,muldefs -pthread -lm -lrt
 ifeq (${LINK_UBSAN},1)
 LIBS += -lubsan
 endif
@@ -89,10 +89,10 @@
 STD_CPPFLAGS = ${STD_CFLAGS}
 STD_CXXFLAGS = ${STD_CFLAGS}
 
-BCM_CFLAGS   ?= -Wall -Werror -fno-strict-aliasing
+BCM_CFLAGS   ?= -Wall -Wno-error -fno-strict-aliasing
 ifeq ($(platform), iproc-3_6)
 ifeq (BE,$(ENDIAN_MODE))
-BCM_CFLAGS   += -Werror=format-security
+BCM_CFLAGS   += -Wno-error=format-security
 endif
 endif
 
